TI  - Uncertainty-Aware Domain Incremental Learning for Gesture Recognition in Robotic IoT Systems
AU  - K. K. Podder
AU  - J. Zhang
AU  - S. Mao
AU  - Z. Yu
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE 11th World Forum on Internet of Things (WF-IoT)
JO  - 2025 IEEE 11th World Forum on Internet of Things (WF-IoT)
SP  - 1
EP  - 7
AB  - Gesture recognition plays a vital role in enabling intuitive, hands-free communication between humans and robotic IoT systems in dynamic and safety-critical environments. From factory floors and construction sites to aircraft decks, gestures offer a universal, silent, and efficient mode of interaction, especially where voice or radio communication is unavailable. A prominent example is air-marshalling, where ground crew and flight-deck teams use standardized hand signals to guide aircraft operations. As robots are increasingly deployed in such scenarios, gesture recognition models must adapt to new environments, characterized by shifting lighting, camera angles, or user variations, without retraining from scratch or compromising safety. This work introduces URDIL, an Uncertainty-aware and Replay-based Domain-Incremental Learning framework for robust gesture recognition in robotic IoT systems. URDIL integrates conformal prediction for real-time uncertainty estimation with a memory-replay mechanism that enables stable, safe, and efficient adaptation to evolving domains. Extensive experiments, using air-marshalling as a case study, demonstrate that URDIL can reliably detect uncertain predictions before adaptation (PUE = 1.0) and achieve high post-adaptation accuracy using only a small annotated subset from the target domain, demonstrating strong cross-domain generalization with minimal forgetting. Together, these results support its promise as a trustworthy learning strategy for gesture-based human–robot interaction in real-world IoT deployments.
