AB  - This study designs a multifunctional intelligent vehicle system for indoor autonomous following, integrating ultra-wideband (UWB) Two-Way Ranging (TWR) positioning, machine-vision fall detection, and lidar-based obstacle avoidance. UWB technology provides strong multipath suppression and submeter accuracy; however, its performance decreases in non-line-of-sight (NLOS) conditions, and most existing systems lack integrated multitask capabilities. To address these issues, the proposed system employs a Raspberry Pi 5B as the core computing platform, a BU03-Kit ultra-wideband (UWB) module as the positioning unit, and an ESP32S3 as the chassis and servo controller. A dual-base-station TWR model with geometric calculations estimates the target angle and distance in real time, while an improved YOLOv5 algorithm performs fall detection and SLAM-assisted lidar enables static and dynamic obstacle avoidance. Experiments show that under line-of-sight conditions, the system achieves a root mean square error below 0.03 m and a 100% tracking success rate with partial target reacquisition in occlusion scenarios. The fall detection sensitivity reached 80% and specificity 100%, and the obstacle avoidance success rates were 100% for static and 80% for dynamic obstacles. These results verify the system’s high accuracy, stability, and application potential in intelligent elderly care and indoor service robots, and provide reference data for UWB and multimodal sensor fusion applications.
