AB  - Falls in assisted living and other elderly care environments occur with varying severity, ranging from instances when older adults require immediate medical assistance to those when they can get up independently and do not need further help. Most conventional fall-detection systems respond to all fall-like events in a similar way, which can lead to high false-alarm rates, unnecessary dispatches, and increased burden on caregivers and emergency responders. This paper presents Fall Assist Companion, an intelligent and adaptive fall-management system for a mobile assistive robot that distinguishes among assisted falls, self-recoverable falls, and floor-level pickup activities and links these event types to distinct robot behaviors. The methodology combined integrated behavioral-environment modeling, context-aware state updates, and operator-based adaptive decision mechanisms to drive a tri-class event process and a hierarchical navigation controller that performed shortest-path routing, feasibility-aware motion, structured approach behavior near the fallen person, and assistance-return sequence. A Monte Carlo evaluation with 108 simulated household trials, each populated with Poisson-distributed assisted and non-assist events, was used to compare the proposed system with a baseline approach that responded uniformly to all fall-like events. Across held-out test trials, the proposed Fall Assist Companion system achieved higher sensitivity to assisted falls (523 vs. 392 true positives) and fewer missed assisted falls (158 vs. 289), while producing significantly lower false-alarm rates on self-recoverable and pickup events (0.34-0.38 for the baseline vs. about 0.11-0.12 for the proposed system). The median per-trial accuracy was higher for the proposed system (0.857 vs. 0.600 for the baseline), and agreement metrics such as Cohen's $\kappa$ and the Matthews correlation coefficient were also higher for the proposed system (approximately 0.6 vs. values near 0.2 for the baseline). The cost-curve and lift-curve analysis further showed lower normalized cost and a more effective ranking of assisted falls. These findings demonstrate that an intelligent, event-aware, context-adaptive robotic system can substantially reduce unnecessary interventions while ensuring reliable assistance for falls in shared human-robot environments for elderly care.
