AB  - Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) is essential for the environmental perception of quadruped robots to develop useful real-world applications. It facilitates advancements in locomotion capabilities of commercially available quadruped robots to improve mobility, safety, and physical health maintenance for senior residents in nursing homes by acting as a walking robot partner equipped with human fall detection.This provides an opportunity to relieve the burden on the nursing staff, but be alerted when a fall is detected and promptly attend the senior resident. However, many state-of-the-art SLAM algorithms are resource-demanding and lack real-time responsiveness, especially for end devices such as the quadruped robot Go1. Furthermore, it is observed that the SLAM parameters are distinct for indoor and outdoor environments that require optimizations independently, especially when translating the required 3D point clouds to 2D to reduce resource demands but capture important surrounding information. In this project, various experiments were conducted on an open source 2D SLAM algorithm -SLAM Toolbox for both indoor and outdoor environments which determined the efficacy of 2D SLAM in outdoor terrains, which are not always flat, and indoor terrains. Furthermore, it was observed that in outdoor terrains, there is a constant trade-off between accuracy and range. As the range increases, the accuracy and precision of the map are diminished, and vice versa. After extensive parameter tuning and testing, benchmark results show a significant improvement in the outdoor map with the 2D SLAM. The results prove that proper tuning and development of 2D SLAM has the potential to yield promising results, which can subsequently be utilized by the walking partner in indoor and outdoor environments. Thereby, providing a more reliable human-robot-interaction for the seniors and nursing staff. To construct SLAM maps, a 3D LiDAR RS-Helios-16P was affixed to the robot dog Unitree Go1, and for odometer data, the in-built IMU available on the robot was employed.
